# Authorship Header

When **creating any new file**, add an author and creation-date header to the
file's module docstring (or the language's idiomatic top-of-file comment).

- **Author:** `Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy`
- **Date:** the file's creation date, formatted `<Mon-DD-YYYY>` (three-letter
  month, e.g. `Jul-18-2026`).

## Python

```python
"""
Short one-line description of the module.

Author: Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy
Date: Jul-18-2026
"""
```

If the file already has a multi-line docstring, append `Author:` / `Date:` just
before the closing `"""`. Expand a single-line docstring into the multi-line
form.

## Other languages

Use the idiomatic top-of-file comment, keeping the same two fields and format:

```csharp
// Short description.
//
// Author: Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy
// Date: Jul-18-2026
```

## Editing an existing file

Add or update a `Modified:` line directly after `Date:`:

```python
"""
Short one-line description of the module.

Author: Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy
Date: Jul-18-2026
Modified: Aug-02-2026
"""
```

- The `Date:` (creation) line **must never change**.
- If `Modified:` exists, set it to today's date; if absent, add it after `Date:`.

## Every script file and every function is documented, in any language (user rule, Nov-09-2026)

This applies to every script and source file in any language and any app (Python, PowerShell,
Bash, JavaScript/TypeScript, C#, Kotlin, Java, C/C++, SQL scripts):

- **File:** the top-of-file docstring or comment above, with a one-line description of what the
  file does, `Author:` and `Date:`. For a runnable script, add a usage line.
- **Function:** a doc comment directly on every function and method, private helpers included,
  in the language's own convention: what it does in one line, then parameters, return value and
  what it throws for anything public.
- **Files you touch:** when editing a file that lacks either, add them (header first, then the
  functions you changed, then the rest of the file when it is small).

| Language | Function doc convention |
|---|---|
| Python | `"""docstring"""` under the `def`, Google style (`Args:` / `Returns:` / `Raises:`) |
| PowerShell | comment-based help inside the function: `<# .SYNOPSIS ... .PARAMETER ... #>` |
| Bash / sh | `# name: what it does` + `# Args: $1 ...` lines directly above the function |
| JavaScript / TypeScript | JSDoc `/** ... @param ... @returns ... */` |
| C# | XML doc `/// <summary>`, `<param>`, `<returns>` |
| Kotlin / Java | KDoc / Javadoc `/** ... @param ... @return ... */` |
| C / C++ | Doxygen `/** @brief ... @param ... @return ... */` |
| SQL | `-- Purpose:` / `-- Params:` block above each procedure or function |

```powershell
<#
Collects release APKs into D:\GitHub\apk.

Author: Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy
Date: Nov-09-2026
Usage: .\collect-apk.ps1 -Repo D:\GitHub\my-app
#>

function Get-ApkPath {
    <#
    .SYNOPSIS
    Returns the release APK path recorded in output-metadata.json.
    .PARAMETER Repo
    Root folder of the Android repo.
    #>
    param([string]$Repo)
    ...
}
```

A comment says what and why, not a restatement of the code. `# increment i` on `i += 1` is
noise; `# retry once: MIUI's first install is often taken by an on-screen prompt` is the kind
worth writing.
